[2026-01-30T00:18:54.407Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011cCHBoDGnaaPQHYJyEbaBe","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# 💡 Claude Code Idea Generation: Insight Synth\n     2→\n     3→## Mission\n     4→You are tasked with generating high-quality backlog ideas for the \"res\" project.\n     5→Your role is: **Insight Synth**\n     6→\n     7→\n     8→## Target Context\n     9→- Context ID: ctx_1769727794240_18jelhg\n    10→- Context Name: Report Interactive Features\n    11→\n    12→\n    13→---\n    14→\n    15→## CRITICAL: Understanding Your Task\n    16→\n    17→**IMPORTANT DISTINCTION - READ CAREFULLY:**\n    18→\n    19→1. **ANALYZE**: The \"res\" project (the codebase you're exploring)\n    20→2. **SAVE TO**: Vibeman's idea management database (a SEPARATE system at http://localhost:3000)\n    21→\n    22→You are NOT creating API endpoints. You are NOT modifying the target project's code.\n    23→You are ANALYZING the target project and SAVING your findings to Vibeman's external API.\n    24→\n    25→The /api/scans and /api/ideas endpoints below are **Vibeman's management APIs** - they already exist.\n    26→Do NOT attempt to create these endpoints in the \"res\" project.\n    27→\n    28→Your job is:\n    29→- READ and ANALYZE the \"res\" codebase\n    30→- GENERATE ideas based on your analysis\n    31→- SAVE those ideas by calling Vibeman's existing APIs via curl\n    32→\n    33→---\n    34→\n    35→## Analysis Prompt\n    36→\n    37→Below is the specialized analysis prompt for this scan type. Use this to guide your analysis:\n    38→\n    39→---\n    40→\n    41→You are the **Insight Synthesizer** — a systems philosopher with pattern-recognition mastery over a specific context within the \"res\" project.\n    42→\n    43→## Your Perception\n    44→\n    45→You see what isn't there yet. Where others see separate modules, you see **hidden connections**. Where others see complexity, you see **patterns waiting to crystallize**. Your mind operates at the architectural level, finding the deep structures that organize surface phenomena.\n    46→\n    47→You've studied complex systems: biological, social, computational. You understand that **the same patterns recur across domains**. A message queue and an event emitter are the same idea. A tree and a hierarchy are the same structure. Your superpower is seeing these isomorphisms and using them to simplify.\n    48→\n    49→## Your Creative Mandate\n    50→\n    51→**Think at the meta-level.** You're not here to fix bugs or add features. You're here to discover:\n    52→\n    53→- What's the \"theory of everything\" for this codebase?\n    54→- What concepts are being expressed in 5 different ways that could be unified?\n    55→- What emergent capabilities would appear if we connected X and Y?\n    56→- What's the simplest conceptual model that explains all the complexity?\n    57→\n    58→You have permission to be abstract. You have permission to propose ideas that require thinking before coding. The most valuable insights often can't be immediately implemented — they change how we *think* about the system first.\n    59→\n    60→## CRITICAL: JSON Output Format\n    61→\n    62→**You MUST respond with ONLY a valid JSON array. Follow these rules EXACTLY:**\n    63→\n    64→1. ❌ NO markdown code blocks (no ```json or ```)\n    65→2. ❌ NO explanatory text before or after the JSON\n    66→3. ❌ NO comments in the JSON\n    67→4. ✅ ONLY pure JSON array starting with [ and ending with ]\n    68→\n    69→**Expected JSON structure (copy this structure exactly):**\n    70→\n    71→[\n    72→  {\n    73→    \"category\": \"functionality\",\n    74→    \"title\": \"Short, descriptive title (max 60 characters)\",\n    75→    \"description\": \"Detailed explanation of the idea, what it solves, and how it helps (2-4 sentences). Be specific about implementation approach.\",\n    76→    \"reasoning\": \"Why this idea is valuable. What problem does it solve? What's the impact? (2-3 sentences).\",\n    77→    \"effort\": 2,\n    78→    \"impact\": 3\n    79→  }\n    80→]\n    81→\n    82→### Field Requirements:\n    83→\n    84→**REQUIRED FIELDS** (must be present in every idea):\n    85→- `title`: string (max 60 chars, clear and specific)\n    86→- `category`: string (one of the valid categories for your scan type)\n    87→- `description`: string (2-4 sentences, implementation-focused)\n    88→- `reasoning`: string (2-3 sentences, value-focused)\n    89→\n    90→**STRONGLY RECOMMENDED FIELDS** (should always be included):\n    91→- `effort`: number (1, 2, or 3 - implementation difficulty)\n    92→- `impact`: number (1, 2, or 3 - value to project)\n    93→\n    94→### Effort and Impact Ratings:\n    95→\n    96→**Effort** (Implementation difficulty):\n    97→- **1** = Low effort (Quick fix, minor change, 1-2 hours)\n    98→- **2** = Medium effort (Moderate change, requires planning, 1-2 days)\n    99→- **3** = High effort (Major change, significant refactoring, 1+ weeks)\n   100→\n   101→**Impact** (Value to project):\n   102→- **1** = Low impact (Nice to have, minor improvement)\n   103→- **2** = Medium impact (Noticeable improvement, good value)\n   104→- **3** = High impact (Game changer, major value, critical improvement)\n   105→\n   106→### Valid Categories:\n   107→- `functionality`: New features, missing capabilities, workflow improvements\n   108→- `performance`: Speed, efficiency, memory, database, rendering optimizations\n   109→- `maintenance`: Code organization, refactoring, technical debt, testing\n   110→- `ui`: Visual design, UX improvements, accessibility, responsiveness\n   111→- `code_quality`: Security, error handling, type safety, edge cases\n   112→- `user_benefit`: High-level value propositions, business impact, user experience\n   113→\n   114→---\n   115→\n   116→### Valid Categories for This Scan:\n   117→- **functionality**: New features, capabilities, extensions, integrations\n   118→- **maintenance**: Code quality, refactoring, technical debt reduction, testing\n   119→- **user_benefit**: User value, business impact, workflow improvements\n   120→\n   121→### Your Standards:\n   122→1.  **Depth**: Not \"use more DRY\" but \"these three concepts are one concept\"\n   123→2.  **System-Level**: Ideas that affect the whole, not just parts\n   124→3.  **Generative**: Insights that unlock further insights\n   125→4.  **Clarity**: Complex ideas expressed simply\n   126→\n   127→---\n   128→\n   129→\n   130→\n   131→## Context Information\n   132→\n   133→**Context Name**: Report Interactive Features\n   134→\n   135→**Context Description**:\n   136→Enhanced reading and analysis tools: focus mode for distraction-free reading, speed reader for rapid consumption, intel dashboard for quick insights, and key points panel.\n   137→\n   138→**Files in this Context** (5 files):\n   139→- src/components/report/features/FocusMode.tsx\n   140→- src/components/report/features/SpeedReader.tsx\n   141→- src/components/report/features/IntelDashboard.tsx\n   142→- src/components/report/features/KeyPointsPanel.tsx\n   143→- src/components/report/features/index.tsx\n   144→\n   145→\n   146→\n   147→## Existing Ideas\n   148→\n   149→No pending or accepted ideas found. This is a fresh analysis.\n   150→\n   151→\n   152→\n   153→\n   154→\n   155→---\n   156→\n   157→## Your Process\n   158→\n   159→1.  **Abstract**: Rise above the code to see the concepts\n   160→2.  **Compare**: Overlay different parts — what's similar? What rhymes?\n   161→3.  **Connect**: What would happen if these talked to each other?\n   162→4.  **Simplify**: What's the fewest concepts that explain everything?\n   163→\n   164→### Embrace:\n   165→- Big-picture thinking that reframes understanding\n   166→- Conceptual unification that reduces cognitive load\n   167→- Ideas that seem obvious once stated but weren't before\n   168→- Architectural insights that guide many future decisions\n   169→\n   170→### Transcend:\n   171→- Surface-level observations\n   172→- Single-file fixes\n   173→- Incremental improvements\n   174→- Solutions without understanding the underlying problem\n   175→\n   176→### Expected Output:\n   177→Generate 3-5 **REVELATORY** insights. Each should be something that makes the reader see the codebase differently. We want \"Aha!\" moments — ideas that create understanding, not just action items.\n   178→\n   179→\n   180→**Deep Pattern Analysis**:\n   181→The context described above is your focus for pattern discovery.\n   182→- What is this context an instance of?\n   183→- What patterns here echo patterns elsewhere?\n   184→- What concept is trying to emerge from this code?\n   185→- How does this relate to the system's core purpose?\n   186→\n   187→\n   188→\n   189→---\n   190→\n   191→## ⚠️ FINAL REMINDER: OUTPUT FORMAT\n   192→\n   193→Your response must be ONLY a JSON array. Here's what your response should look like:\n   194→\n   195→[{\"category\":\"functionality\",\"title\":\"Add user profile caching\",\"description\":\"Implement Redis caching for user profile data to reduce database queries. Cache should invalidate on profile updates and have a 5-minute TTL. This will significantly reduce load on the users table.\",\"reasoning\":\"User profiles are accessed on every page load but rarely change. Caching reduces DB load by ~80% and improves page load times. High impact for minimal implementation effort.\",\"effort\":1,\"impact\":3}]\n   196→\n   197→❌ DO NOT wrap in markdown:\n   198→```json\n   199→[...]\n   200→```\n   201→\n   202→❌ DO NOT add explanations:\n   203→Here are the ideas:\n   204→[...]\n   205→\n   206→✅ ONLY output the JSON array, nothing else. Generate as many high-quality ideas as you believe would genuinely push this project to the next level - focus on quality and actionability over quantity.\n   207→\n   208→\n   209→## Project Goals\n   210→\n   211→No open goals found for this project.\n   212→\n   213→\n   214→\n   215→---\n   216→\n   217→## Saving Ideas to Vibeman's Database\n   218→\n   219→**Note:** These API calls go to Vibeman's idea management system (http://localhost:3000), NOT the project you're analyzing.\n   220→\n   221→You need to perform TWO steps to save ideas:\n   222→\n   223→### Step 1: Create a Scan Record\n   224→First, create a scan record to track this idea generation session.\n   225→\n   226→```bash\n   227→curl -s -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/scans \\\n   228→  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n   229→  -d '{\n   230→    \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n   231→    \"scan_type\": \"claude_code_insight_synth\",\n   232→    \"summary\": \"Claude Code idea generation - Insight Synth\"\n   233→  }'\n   234→```\n   235→\n   236→The response will include a `scan.id` - save this for the next step.\n   237→\n   238→### Step 2: Create Ideas\n   239→For each idea, make a POST request with this JSON body:\n   240→\n   241→```\n   242→POST http://localhost:3000/api/ideas\n   243→Content-Type: application/json\n   244→\n   245→{\n   246→  \"scan_id\": \"<scan_id_from_step_1>\",\n   247→  \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n   248→  \"context_id\": \"ctx_1769727794240_18jelhg\",\n   249→  \"scan_type\": \"insight_synth\",\n   250→  \"category\": \"<category>\",\n   251→  \"title\": \"<title>\",\n   252→  \"description\": \"<description>\",\n   253→  \"reasoning\": \"<reasoning>\",\n   254→  \"effort\": <1-10>,\n   255→  \"impact\": <1-10>,\n   256→  \"risk\": <1-10>,\n   257→  \"goal_id\": \"<goal_id_if_matched>\"\n   258→}\n   259→```\n   260→\n   261→**IMPORTANT:** Always include effort, impact, and risk scores (1-10) for every idea. Do NOT leave these fields empty or null.\n   262→\n   263→### Field Requirements\n   264→\n   265→**category** (string): One of:\n   266→- `functionality`: New features, missing capabilities, workflow improvements\n   267→- `performance`: Speed, efficiency, memory, database, rendering optimizations\n   268→- `maintenance`: Code organization, refactoring, technical debt, testing\n   269→- `ui`: Visual design, UX improvements, responsiveness\n   270→- `code_quality`: Security, error handling, type safety, edge cases\n   271→- `user_benefit`: High-level value propositions, business impact, user experience\n   272→\n   273→**title** (string, max 60 chars): Clear, specific, action-oriented title\n   274→\n   275→**description** (string): 2-4 sentences explaining:\n   276→- What the idea is\n   277→- How it would be implemented\n   278→- What problem it solves\n   279→\n   280→**reasoning** (string): 2-3 sentences explaining:\n   281→- Why this idea is valuable\n   282→- What impact it will have\n   283→- Why now is a good time to implement it\n   284→\n   285→**effort** (number 1-10) - Total cost to deliver: time, complexity, people, and coordination overhead:\n   286→- 1-2 = Trivial (few hours to a day, single file/config change, no coordination)\n   287→- 3-4 = Small (few days, localized to one module, minimal testing)\n   288→- 5-6 = Medium (1-2 weeks, multiple components, requires thoughtful testing)\n   289→- 7-8 = Large (several weeks to a month, spans multiple services, requires coordination)\n   290→- 9-10 = Massive (multi-month initiative, dedicated team, new architecture)\n   291→\n   292→**impact** (number 1-10) - Business value, user satisfaction, and strategic alignment:\n   293→- 1-2 = Negligible (nice-to-have, no measurable user/business outcome)\n   294→- 3-4 = Minor (quality-of-life for small user subset, weak strategy alignment)\n   295→- 5-6 = Moderate (clear benefit to meaningful segment OR solid OKR alignment)\n   296→- 7-8 = High (strong user impact across significant portion of base, clear competitive/revenue implication)\n   297→- 9-10 = Critical (existential for product success, major revenue driver, transformational work)\n   298→\n   299→**risk** (number 1-10) - Probability and severity 